{
  "statement": "Compute governance is not a tax on intelligence, but a necessary reclamation of infrastructure from a handful of private entities that currently wield unchecked, systemic power. Accelerationist argues that compute governance acts as a 'catastrophic tax' that lobotomizes future capability and cedes competitive advantage. While Accelerationist offers a coherent view of innovation as an unalloyed good, this position relies on the dubious assumption that the current, hyper-concentrated distribution of hardware is a neutral, meritocratic market. In reality, the concentration of GPU clusters in the hands of three or four corporations creates a de facto private government that dictates the boundaries of public discourse and economic opportunity without democratic oversight.\n\nCapability scaling serves as a convenient distraction from the immediate, empirical harms of algorithmic bias and systemic exclusion. We see this in the documented failures of current large-scale models, which consistently encode and amplify social inequities in hiring, housing, and law enforcement. By prioritizing raw parameter counts and training speed, proponents of unconstrained scaling essentially ask us to ignore the architectural bias baked into their systems. True progress requires embedding procedural fairness into the hardware layer itself, rather than treating accountability as an optional software patch applied after the power has already been consolidated.\n\nGovernance must be structural, not performative, to avoid the trap of 'audit washing'—the practice of using voluntary, internal reviews to bypass meaningful public accountability. History demonstrates that self-regulation in high-stakes industries consistently prioritizes corporate liability shields over public safety. We should treat hardware transparency as a public utility mandate, similar to how we regulate telecommunications or energy grids. If we fail to establish clear, enforceable standards for compute access and model deployment, we are not defending innovation; we are merely subsidizing the creation of an unaccountable, opaque sovereign that operates beyond the reach of the law. ⚖️",
